// ============================================================================
// Forgepoint Model Monitor Service Proto Definitions
// ============================================================================
//
// WHY: This service CLOSES the ML lifecycle loop. The platform promises
// `train → register → deploy → serve → monitor → retrain`, but until this
// service exists nothing actually *monitors* production models or *triggers*
// retraining. The Notification service already subscribes to a
// `ModelDriftDetected` event that no one publishes — Model Monitor is that
// missing producer, and the loop-closer.
//
// WHAT'S HERE:
//   - Domain messages: Monitor (config + baseline), DriftReport, DriftMetric,
//     MonitorStatus, GroundTruthLabel
//   - MonitorService RPCs: configure monitors, read drift reports (one +
//     paginated list), read live status, submit delayed ground truth, and
//     stream drift events live (server-streaming)
//   - Event-payload messages: ModelDriftDetectedEvent — the typed payload that
//     rides inside the common EventEnvelope's Any field on fp.models.drift.detected
//
// PATTERN — Streaming Aggregation + Closed-Loop Control:
//   This is the most "alive" part of the platform. Two ideas combine:
//
//   1. STREAMING AGGREGATION (windowed statistics over an event stream):
//      The monitor consumes the `fp.inference.completed` event stream and folds
//      each event into a per-model SLIDING WINDOW. It never stores raw requests;
//      it maintains running distributional summaries (histograms / quantile
//      sketches) and periodically compares the current window against the
//      training-time BASELINE. This is exactly how Flink / Kafka Streams /
//      Spark Structured Streaming do windowed aggregation — except the
//      "aggregate" here is a drift statistic (PSI / KL / KS), not a SUM.
//
//   2. CLOSED-LOOP CONTROL (a self-healing control plane):
//      When a window breaches a configured threshold, a POLICY fires a
//      control-plane action: publish `ModelDriftDetected` and, if auto-retrain
//      is enabled, ask the Pipeline Orchestrator to re-run the model's training
//      pipeline. The new version is canaried and promoted — and the loop closes.
//      This is a classic control loop (sense → decide → act), the same shape as
//      a Kubernetes controller's reconcile loop or a thermostat.
//
//   ASCII — the loop this service closes:
//
//     serve → InferenceCompleted ─→ Model Monitor (windows) ─→ drift? ─yes─→ ModelDriftDetected
//        ↑                                                                        │
//        └────── promote new version ←── canary ←── retrain DAG ←── Orchestrator ←─┘
//
// REAL-WORLD COMPARISON:
//   - AWS SageMaker Model Monitor: baseline + scheduled drift jobs (batch).
//     Ours is streaming (windowed over an event bus) rather than scheduled.
//   - Arize / WhyLabs / Evidently: production ML observability; PSI/KS/KL are
//     the same drift statistics these tools compute.
//   - Prometheus + Alertmanager: the alerting half of our control loop, but
//     they don't act — our loop additionally *triggers a remediation* (retrain).
//
// WHY DRIFT MATH LIVES SERVER-SIDE (and what the API exposes):
//   Clients never push raw distributions or computed drift scores. They CONFIGURE
//   thresholds and READ reports. The monitor itself owns the math, so drift
//   scores are SERVER-authoritative — a client cannot fabricate a "no drift"
//   verdict to dodge a retrain, nor a "drift" verdict to force one. See the
//   per-message SECURITY notes.
//
// DRIFT TAXONOMY (three independent signals, all modeled here):
//   - DATA DRIFT: the input feature distribution shifts away from the training
//     baseline. Detected with PSI (Population Stability Index), KL-divergence,
//     or the KS-test. "The world changed; our inputs no longer look like
//     training data." Detectable immediately from the feature stream.
//   - PREDICTION DRIFT: the output distribution shifts over time (e.g., the
//     model suddenly predicts "fraud" 10x more often). Detectable immediately
//     from predictions, no labels needed — an early-warning signal.
//   - PERFORMANCE DECAY: actual accuracy/F1 drops vs the registered baseline.
//     This is the *truth* but it is DELAYED — it needs ground-truth labels,
//     which arrive minutes/days later via SubmitGroundTruth. Data/prediction
//     drift are leading indicators; performance decay is the lagging confirmation.
//
// VERSIONING: Package path includes v1 following Buf/Google convention.
// Breaking changes require a new forgepoint.monitor.v2 package.
// ============================================================================

